WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:01.920
(audience cheering)
- Today is a very special hour

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.020
as we celebrate the final season of one

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.907
of the most fun and clever
shows of the last decade,

00:00:06.907 --> 00:00:09.699
"The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel."
(audience cheering)

00:00:09.699 --> 00:00:11.280
Exactly.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.100
Everyone involved has won
numerous awards for their work

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.540
on the show 'cause it's an excellent show.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.430
You can see the fifth and final season

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.040
on Prime Video with new episodes weekly.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.599
From "The Marvelous Mrs.
Maisel" please welcome

00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:24.417
Rachel Brosnahan,
(audience cheering)

00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:26.481
Alex Borstein, Tony Shalhoub, Michael Zegen.

00:00:26.481 --> 00:00:30.398
(cheerful upbeat music begins)

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:37.417
(audience cheering)

00:00:41.327 --> 00:00:45.494
(cheerful upbeat music continues)

00:00:50.237 --> 00:00:53.320
(audience cheering)

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:59.298
What's up, y'all? Get it
out, get it out. (laughs)

00:00:59.298 --> 00:01:01.895
(audience cheering)

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:02.850
We're so excited to have you here.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.680
It's the fifth and final,
I've had you here before.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.919
Welcome to the show, everybody.

00:01:06.919 --> 00:01:08.338
- I think I was here virtually,
no, we were on the roof.

00:01:08.338 --> 00:01:10.418
- We were on the roof. We roof party.

00:01:10.418 --> 00:01:11.639
(Rachel laughing)

00:01:11.639 --> 00:01:14.224
So did did any of you know
each other before this show?

00:01:14.224 --> 00:01:15.057
- We did.

00:01:15.057 --> 00:01:16.335
- I saw Tony in a lot of movies.

00:01:16.335 --> 00:01:17.919
(audience laughing)

00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:18.752
- I knew Alex a little bit.

00:01:18.752 --> 00:01:19.980
- [Alex] We met on Mad TV.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.110
- Right.
- We met on Mad TV.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.250
- They asked me do an episode of Mad TV,

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.737
I was a massive fan of that show.

00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:27.420
- And I was a massive fan of his

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.694
and I had a little secret crush on him.

00:01:29.694 --> 00:01:31.437
(Kelly awwing)

00:01:31.437 --> 00:01:32.439
- Wasn't that big a secret.

00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:34.560
She would hang out backstage.
(audience laughing)

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.687
- I'm not subtle.

00:01:36.956 --> 00:01:38.812
- [Kelly] Subtle. (laughing)

00:01:38.812 --> 00:01:40.140
- Yeah, so we met way back when.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.108
And he looks exactly the
same, which is not fair.

00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:45.028
(overlapping chatter)

00:01:45.028 --> 00:01:45.990
- Yeah, you do have that thing,

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.930
the fountain of the same thing, the youth.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.388
You look the same.

00:01:50.388 --> 00:01:51.221
- Fountain of cosmetic surgery?

00:01:51.221 --> 00:01:53.220
- No, no, no, no, no.
(audience laughing)

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.348
Well, I have to--
- Benjamin Button over here.

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:57.128
- Yeah, aging backwards

00:01:57.128 --> 00:01:57.961
- Also, we knew each other before.

00:01:57.961 --> 00:02:00.510
Michael and I met when I was 19.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:01.343
- [Kelly] Oh wow.

00:02:01.343 --> 00:02:03.708
- It's not as salacious as you think.

00:02:03.708 --> 00:02:05.409
(audience laughing)

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:06.513
- When we were teenagers...

00:02:06.513 --> 00:02:09.750
- Well we did a reading,
we did a play reading.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.320
And I remember walking
you back to the subway

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.730
'cause you were going back to NYU.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.800
- You walked me to the
subway? That's so nice.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.007
- She remembered it too.
- Which I also remember.

00:02:20.007 --> 00:02:21.030
(Alex laughing)

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.830
- That's so nice. That is nice.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.960
So I have to ask, what was
the last day on set like?

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.180
Because that's a big deal.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.013
- Yeah, it was awful, it was awful.

00:02:28.013 --> 00:02:30.180
- [Rachel] It was terrible. (laughs)

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.780
- A lot of tears.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.890
- It was painful. I mean, we
just all love the show so much.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.910
We love the work and we just
couldn't quite wrap our brains

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.943
around the fact that it was,
you know, coming to a close.

00:02:43.539 --> 00:02:44.820
- I feel like sometimes it
doesn't hit you till afterwards.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:49.170
- Yeah, we had been, I mean,
Alex is a picture of stoicism

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.850
most days on set. (laughs)

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.175
- Yeah, I'm usually very
cold and, well, kind of mean.

00:02:54.175 --> 00:02:55.230
(Kelly laughing)

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.690
- I saw the other word
flash through your eyes.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:00.140
- She kept it daytime.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.081
- For Kelly Clarkson, it's
daytime, it's daytime.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.079
(Kelly laughing)

00:03:03.079 --> 00:03:04.198
- Don't worry, they have
to bleep me all the time.

00:03:04.198 --> 00:03:05.201
You're fine.

00:03:05.201 --> 00:03:06.720
- But we had to rehearse
one of our scenes early

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.330
that morning on the
last day, and we came in

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.500
and sort of stood across from each other

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.750
and we were starting to rehearse
and Alex was getting teary

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.220
at like eight o'clock in the morning.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.241
- I couldn't look at
her. I just looked away.

00:03:16.241 --> 00:03:17.074
- Could not look me in the eyes.

00:03:17.074 --> 00:03:18.270
And then I couldn't look her in the eyes.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.537
And so we spent all of our
rehearsals kind of looking,

00:03:20.537 --> 00:03:21.370
I was thinking, I was
looking at the center

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:23.176
of your forehead and you
were looking at my...

00:03:23.176 --> 00:03:24.009
(audience laughing)

00:03:24.009 --> 00:03:24.842
- To be fair, isn't that usually

00:03:24.842 --> 00:03:25.830
where you have to look at me?

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.840
You're right.
(audience laughing)

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.570
- So Michael, did you get to
keep this super creepy doll?

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.223
I wanna show everyone this doll.

00:03:33.419 --> 00:03:34.252
(Michael laughing)

00:03:34.252 --> 00:03:36.300
- [Rachel] Our daughter.
That's our daughter, Kelly.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.570
- I have adopted that super creepy doll.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.550
She lives with me now.
(Kelly laughing)

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.996
No, no, no, no. No, I left her behind.

00:03:44.996 --> 00:03:46.628
(Kelly laughing)

00:03:46.628 --> 00:03:48.420
Very creepy, sticky doll.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:49.740
- It would've just been
fun if you took it, though,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.420
and then you kept it,
like, in your guest bedroom

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.985
for when people came over,
just randomly in a corner.

00:03:54.985 --> 00:03:55.818
- It'd be nice to have a guest bedroom,

00:03:55.818 --> 00:03:57.475
but I live in New York.
(audience laughing)

00:03:57.475 --> 00:04:00.225
(Kelly laughing)

00:04:01.775 --> 00:04:03.439
- [Alex] Nice flex, Kelly.

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:05.295
- I'm so sorry. (laughs)

00:04:05.295 --> 00:04:06.240
Oh, I just, you know why I went there is

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.270
I stayed at, that's gonna
sound like a flex too,

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.620
I stayed at someone's house.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:11.955
- [Alex] Who's house, Kelly?

00:04:11.955 --> 00:04:14.430
- No, and they had a doll.
- Out with it, Kelly.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.641
- It was Reba and I stayed at her house.

00:04:16.641 --> 00:04:17.474
(audience laughing)

00:04:17.474 --> 00:04:19.840
She had a doll though,
it was just this doll.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.260
And it was so creepy to me.
Just, dolls are creepy to me.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.891
And it was maybe a family
thing, I don't know what it was.

00:04:24.891 --> 00:04:25.724
But then like two days later, she's like,

00:04:25.724 --> 00:04:26.850
"So we found the doll in the closet"

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:28.871
and I'm like, "Oh my God, I'm
sorry I didn't put it back."

00:04:28.871 --> 00:04:30.116
(audience laughing)

00:04:30.116 --> 00:04:31.290
It scared me and I couldn't sleep.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.610
I was just like, "I'm not gonna sleep

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:33.956
with that thing looking at me."

00:04:33.956 --> 00:04:34.975
(audience laughing)

00:04:34.975 --> 00:04:35.808
- I've done that at Airbnbs.

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:36.641
I've had that happen at Airbnbs.

00:04:36.641 --> 00:04:39.030
They had something very
creepy and I can't look at it.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.680
- She was like, "Did you
put my stuff in the closet?"

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.373
I was like, "I kind of did."
(audience laughing)

00:04:42.373 --> 00:04:43.830
I did, I did, I'm so sorry.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:45.210
I should have brought it back out. Anyway.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.800
- I took some ties, you know.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.417
- [Kelly] See? Klepto, I like it.

00:04:49.417 --> 00:04:50.340
(audience laughing)

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.430
- Some of Joel's ties, they're
vintage ties and really cool.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.817
So I took about 20 of them.
(audience laughing)

00:04:55.817 --> 00:04:57.300
- I mean, speaking of
that though, the vintage--

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.320
- I know, I'm sorry.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.350
I hope Donna the costume
designer's not watching.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.180
But yes, I have them.
(Kelly laughing)

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.400
- Speaking of costume design,
it's incredible, this show.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.950
That's why, the acting is so impeccable.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.050
The writing is great. The storytelling.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.900
The wardrobe, the art
department that makes all those,

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.670
you just really feel like
you're there in time.

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.770
Speaking of costume design, it's so cool.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.600
Like, it's such a great team you're in.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.370
That's why I know you're crying at 8 a.m.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:21.510
'Cause it's, it's a great team

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.976
and that doesn't always
happen, like, you know?

00:05:23.976 --> 00:05:25.940
- It doesn't. It was a rare, rare thing.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:26.937
(audience applauding)

00:05:26.937 --> 00:05:28.830
- So going back to the
beginning of, well, everything.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:31.470
Tony, one of your many sisters
got you into acting, right?

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:32.303
- Right.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.150
One of my older sisters was in high school

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:39.300
and they were doing a musical,
they were doing "King and I,"

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.500
and I was recruited.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:42.630
They asked, I guess for them all to bring

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.690
their younger siblings
to play the children.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:50.690
And I wasn't exactly asked,
but I was kind of recruited.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.545
There were handcuffs.

00:05:53.545 --> 00:05:54.378
- Oh my God, I thought
you were gonna be like,

00:05:54.378 --> 00:05:56.061
"I just kinda walked up there."

00:05:56.061 --> 00:05:58.320
(audience laughing)
- No, no.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.250
I was recruited, and
yeah, that was kind of

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.280
my first exposure to the stage.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.250
- That's exciting. Because
you didn't know it was coming.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:11.100
That fresh feeling.
- I was six years old.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:12.150
(Kelly awwing)

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.590
- Rachel, how did your parents feel

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:16.440
when you told them that you wanted to act?

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.241
- They were horrified.
Supremely disappointed.

00:06:19.241 --> 00:06:20.280
(Kelly laughing)

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.740
No, I think they were holding out hope

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.620
until probably about five
minutes ago that maybe

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.401
I was gonna become a
lawyer or something useful

00:06:28.401 --> 00:06:30.600
and contribute to society in some way.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.990
No, they were, I think they
just, they were nervous.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.390
You know, it's an impossible job.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.785
- It's a hard one at that.

00:06:37.785 --> 00:06:39.180
- We don't this because you're
well adjusted and normal

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.920
and it's a really difficult
thing to have success at.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.680
- Yeah, but Alex, you started in standup.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.230
And when was the first
time you got up on stage

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:50.130
and kind of got that bug?

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.020
- I know, it's a little, I don't know

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.110
if you can actually call
it standup, but I was 16

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.590
and I did stand up at this bar. (laughs)

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.884
At a bar that was willing to put me up.

00:07:00.884 --> 00:07:02.730
And so my parents had to come
'cause I wasn't old enough

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.830
to even be in the bar.
(audience laughing)

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.290
And they paid me $20 in cash.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:10.361
And I can tell you--
- $20 in cash. (laughs)

00:07:10.361 --> 00:07:12.320
(audience laughing)

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:13.844
- I've only been paid,
that's only the second time

00:07:13.844 --> 00:07:15.444
in my life I was ever paid to do standup.

00:07:15.444 --> 00:07:16.410
(Kelly laughing)
Like, I'm not joking.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:20.280
Like, from then on, I did
that little gig, and then

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.823
every other thing I did was free open,

00:07:22.823 --> 00:07:25.785
open mic, open mic, open
mic, no one paying, you know.

00:07:25.785 --> 00:07:28.170
And it was a very good
lesson to learn very early.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.600
But you kept that $20 bill.
- I do still have it.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.433
- [Kelly] You do?

00:07:31.433 --> 00:07:32.804
- Yeah. It's in a little frame.

00:07:32.804 --> 00:07:33.820
- [Kelly] That is so cool!

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:34.653
(audience cheering)

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:36.480
- I kept it.
- I love that.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.390
So now you've got a new standup
special coming out, right?

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.870
- I do, I do. It's a comedy special.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:45.647
Maybe we should call it,
like, a filthy TED talk.

00:07:45.647 --> 00:07:46.480
(audience laughing)

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.660
- That is the best teaser.
(laughs) That's great.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.240
- It's kind of a filthy,
there's lessons to be learned.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:54.513
There's storytelling, there's
music, there's singing.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:00.620
It's a deeply personal, wildly fictitious,

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:03.807
filthy account of my life.

00:08:03.807 --> 00:08:05.471
(audience laughing and cheering)

00:08:05.471 --> 00:08:06.570
- Don't you wanna watch it now?

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:11.040
- It's amazing. We saw it
live, it's so, so good.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.526
- It's on Amazon Prime, shock, surprise.

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:15.046
(audience cheering)

00:08:15.046 --> 00:08:18.210
- [Michael] You shot it in the Wolford,

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:22.170
which was the burlesque theater
that we used on our set.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.080
- Yeah, before they tore it down I begged

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:26.460
and I was like, "Can I use it?"

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.410
And Amy was like, "Of course."

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:29.880
So it's kind of special.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.880
Like, nothing else has ever
been shot there aside from--

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.070
- That's not kind of special.
That's really rad, yeah.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:38.070
So wait, so Michael, do you
remember your first performance?

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:40.800
- I mean, I wanted to be an actor

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.120
since I was, like, five years old.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:45.612
But I mean, so there were a few firsts.

00:08:45.612 --> 00:08:47.910
I wrote my fifth grade play.
- What?

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.164
- Yeah, I wrote my fifth
grade play. It was--

00:08:50.164 --> 00:08:53.028
- [Kelly] Ahead of your time. (laughs)

00:08:53.028 --> 00:08:54.990
- It was a play about
aliens coming down to earth

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.951
and these kids teaching them
about the Bill of Rights.

00:08:57.951 --> 00:08:59.400
(audience laughing)

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.310
- Trying to make it interesting.
Make history awesome.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.440
- It didn't go very well, to be honest.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.240
A kid forgot a line and there
was a five minute pause.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.247
(audience awwing)

00:09:09.247 --> 00:09:11.087
- [Kelly] Oh my god, this is adorable!

00:09:11.087 --> 00:09:12.857
(audience applauding)

00:09:12.857 --> 00:09:14.460
- [Michael] That's a different play.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:15.300
That was a different one.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.583
- [Rachel] Very serious actor, Michael.

00:09:17.567 --> 00:09:18.862
- [Michael] That's me in
high school, by the way.

00:09:18.862 --> 00:09:20.010
- I know, oh my God.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.521
I love that you tell this
kind of story. (laughs)

00:09:22.521 --> 00:09:24.270
(audience awwing)

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.160
- [Michael] That was "Cats".
I was Rum Tum Tugger.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.680
- Oh my God, I love you!
(audience laughing)

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.230
I do love when everybody,
everybody always thinks,

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:32.490
you know, they've made
it right off the bat.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.880
Nobody sees that groundwork
and the hustle to pay bills

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.320
while you're trying to get your dream.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.080
So did any of you have any
random jobs before this?

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.820
Like, what did you do before acting?

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.740
- I definitely did.
- I did, yeah.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.120
- What was yours?

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:47.250
- I worked as, you know, dishwashers,

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.830
bus boys, waiters many times.

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:53.580
But the coolest job I had was,
I worked on a garbage truck.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:54.413
- You did?

00:09:54.413 --> 00:09:57.360
- Yeah, it was like a
privately owned company,

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.280
not a municipal company.
(Kelly laughing)

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.930
It was like a resort area.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:01.902
- [Kelly] It was fancy garbage. I like it.

00:10:01.902 --> 00:10:02.735
- It was fancy.
(audience laughing)

00:10:02.735 --> 00:10:04.661
It was the best job.
- I like it.

00:10:04.661 --> 00:10:05.675
(Rachel laughing)

00:10:05.675 --> 00:10:06.960
- Because you're not stuck inside.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:07.950
- That's true. You're not.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:11.370
- And we like wear like
these funky bib overalls

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:13.140
and hang on the back of the truck.

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:14.430
- How long did you do that?
(Kelly laughing)

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:16.470
- It was a summer job.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:17.310
- I'm gonna be honest with you,

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:18.900
one of my things that
I did when I was a kid

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:21.390
to help my stepdad out was we
would always go to the dump

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:24.150
to like, take stuff, I loved it.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:27.450
It was like treasure. I
mean, you know, it was fun.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:30.958
- I got a lot of cool
things on my route, yeah.

00:10:30.958 --> 00:10:31.791
(audience laughing)
- Exactly.

00:10:31.791 --> 00:10:34.530
You're like, "That couch
isn't that bad." (laughs)

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.060
It's in your living room in a minute.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.610
Okay, so what other jobs did y'all have?

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:42.810
- I worked, well, way back
when I did telemarketing,

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.415
I apologized to everyone.
(audience laughing)

00:10:45.415 --> 00:10:46.455
- [Rachel] I have telemarketed as well.

00:10:46.455 --> 00:10:47.452
- [Kelly] You did?

00:10:47.452 --> 00:10:48.455
- I did telemarketing.

00:10:48.455 --> 00:10:50.040
And it was to sell photos,
like, family portraits.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:52.320
And at one point, and I'd
sold none, I'd sold none.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.270
We were locked in a dark
room, there were no windows.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.250
I think people were even allowed to smoke,

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.680
like, in the closed room,
this is how old I am.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.867
And at one point the
manager was kind of like,

00:11:01.867 --> 00:11:04.860
"The person who sells next
is gonna get 100% bonus"

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.833
or something so I called my parents.

00:11:06.833 --> 00:11:08.113
(audience laughing)

00:11:08.113 --> 00:11:10.425
- That's just smart, that's smart.

00:11:10.425 --> 00:11:12.633
- And I sold them a portrait.

00:11:12.633 --> 00:11:14.451
And I still have that
photo of my mother and I

00:11:14.451 --> 00:11:16.841
and it's the worst thing you've ever seen.

00:11:16.841 --> 00:11:17.674
(Kelly laughing)

00:11:17.674 --> 00:11:21.540
Ever. I have like a big
perm and acrylic nails

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:25.011
with, I think, like,
airbrushed dragons on them.

00:11:25.011 --> 00:11:25.844
(Rachel laughing)

00:11:25.844 --> 00:11:27.150
- You just described what I looked like

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.160
in my glamour shot when I was, like, 16.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.249
- That's basically what it was.

00:11:31.249 --> 00:11:32.082
- You've really been holding out on us.

00:11:32.082 --> 00:11:33.420
We've gotta see this photo. (laughs)

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.672
- That was a good one.
I will send it to you.

00:11:35.672 --> 00:11:36.505
(Rachel laughing)

00:11:36.505 --> 00:11:37.980
- [Kelly] You're welcome in advance.

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:40.920
- I worked at a theme restaurant

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.870
called Jekyll and Hyde in New York.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:47.160
And the owner or the manager
came up to me and he had, like,

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.934
a stack of menus and
he was, like, you know,

00:11:49.934 --> 00:11:52.417
it wasn't busy that day and he was like,

00:11:52.417 --> 00:11:55.433
"Take these menus and
wash them." And I left.

00:11:55.433 --> 00:11:58.505
(audience laughing)
- You walked out?

00:11:58.505 --> 00:12:00.390
- I was like, "This isn't my job."

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:01.257
And I hated it in any way and I left.

00:12:01.257 --> 00:12:02.760
And I never got a call from them.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:05.030
Nobody tried finding out where I was.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.254
(audience laughing)
If I was okay.

00:12:07.254 --> 00:12:10.890
- You could have a Dateline
special. Nobody cares.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.483
- They hated you as
much as you hated them.

00:12:12.483 --> 00:12:13.410
- I think the turnover at
Jekyll and Hyde was pretty high.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.553
- It might have been
their way of firing you.

00:12:16.553 --> 00:12:17.640
(audience laughing)

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:19.315
- [Kelly] Let's see
what he'll do. (laughs)

00:12:19.315 --> 00:12:21.150
- I was due.
- "Don't you know who I am?

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:24.798
I'm Michael. I taught the
aliens the Bill of Rights."

00:12:24.798 --> 00:12:25.795
(audience laughing)

00:12:25.795 --> 00:12:28.140
- "You must have missed my
play in fifth grade." (laughs)

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.534
Would've been amazing. (laughs)

00:12:29.534 --> 00:12:30.367
(audience applauding)

00:12:30.367 --> 00:12:32.940
So let's talk about what
happens in the final season.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:33.840
Michael, I'm gonna start with you.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:35.370
Like, where's your character at?

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.790
- You know, this season
we do some flash forwards

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:42.510
instead of flashbacks
and so I think people

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:45.483
are gonna be surprised
to see where Joel is.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:47.820
That's all I can really say.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.200
- Okay, okay.
(Michael laughing)

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.940
I like that tease. All
right, what about you, Tony?

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.358
What about your character?

00:12:52.358 --> 00:12:54.360
- Yeah, I guess the biggest
shift for my character is,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:56.523
you know, when the show started out,

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:01.710
Abe was this sort of, like,
a man of his time where,

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:03.840
you know, his grandchildren
were kind of, like,

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.930
children are to be seen and not heard

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.240
and he was kind of dismissive of them.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:13.290
But by the middle or end of
season five, he's really,

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.650
his main focus, really,
is on these grandchildren.

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:20.670
So I thought that was kind of
an interesting arc for him.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:22.627
- Not too dissimilar to your real life.

00:13:22.627 --> 00:13:23.460
- And I was gonna say.
- And in my real life.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:26.700
- Because he has the most
beautiful grandchildren

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:28.860
I have ever seen, ever.
(audience awwing)

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:30.570
- And also it's worth mentioning

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:33.402
I'm like one of the youngest grandfathers.

00:13:33.402 --> 00:13:35.070
(audience laughing)

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:37.540
- And also it's worth
mentioning I'm a zaddy.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:39.420
(audience laughing)

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.550
That's amazing. (laughs)

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:44.460
All right, Alex, how about
you? Where's your character at?

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.320
- You know, my favorite
thing about Susie is that

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:49.882
she kind of started like a
balled up armadillo, you know?

00:13:49.882 --> 00:13:50.940
(Kelly laughing)

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.460
And every season she just kind of has,

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:56.643
Amy's, like, cracked it open
a little bit unwillingly.

00:13:56.643 --> 00:14:01.080
You learn more and the vulnerability
just kind of spills out

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:06.080
and she doesn't necessarily go up,

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.410
you know, on a trajectory.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:09.150
She kind of opens and it comes out.

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:11.419
She goes in and it's fascinating to me.

00:14:11.419 --> 00:14:12.252
- [Kelly] Get to see the layers.

00:14:12.252 --> 00:14:14.160
- Gets vulnerable and the layers open.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.900
- Is it kind of fun 'cause
it's challenging though?

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:16.733
- Yeah.

00:14:16.733 --> 00:14:18.180
- You're having to show
different sides of your character

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.838
that we maybe haven't seen before?

00:14:19.838 --> 00:14:20.671
- Absolutely. Yeah.

00:14:20.671 --> 00:14:22.470
- So Rachel, where's your character at?

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:26.600
- So, I mean, I feel like this
season shows Midge and Susie,

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.040
this is their make it or break it year.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.590
They have hit rock bottom,
they've gone back and forth.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:34.503
They've taken 10 steps
forward and 25 steps back.

00:14:35.619 --> 00:14:38.400
And Midge has also been
humbled enough at this point

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:39.870
as someone who had experienced

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:43.173
very few instances of humbling
when we met her in the pilot.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.640
To realize that maybe she's
been taking Susie for granted,

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:49.800
she's been taking their partnership
for granted in some ways

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.320
and she owes it to herself
and to the people around her

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.440
that she's put through this
incredible and crazy journey

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:59.820
alongside her to buckle down
and get her done, as they say.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:03.737
And so I think she puts that faith

00:15:03.737 --> 00:15:08.040
really permanently and
forcefully in Susie.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.660
And the two of them kinda link arms

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:10.800
and figure out how
they're gonna get there.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:15.630
And the route is, as you
can expect, not so linear.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:16.560
A little bit surprising.

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.212
- That's what makes it interesting.

00:15:18.212 --> 00:15:19.050
- There's some shenanigans along the way.

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:21.753
We ice skate. There's
Maisel on ice skates.

00:15:21.753 --> 00:15:22.751
- [Kelly] You ice skate?

00:15:22.751 --> 00:15:23.584
- We ice skate this season.

00:15:23.584 --> 00:15:24.417
- Wait, have you ever done that?

00:15:24.417 --> 00:15:26.130
Are you one of those
actors that tells everybody

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:27.720
you can do it and you get
there and you're like, "huh?"

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.030
- Yes, absolutely yes.
(audience laughing)

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.057
- No, that's real.
(Alex laughing)

00:15:32.057 --> 00:15:32.970
That's real. I could not do that.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:36.093
- Alex is really ice skating.
Shock of everyone's lives.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:38.490
- You were an ice skater.

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:41.425
- I was a childhood ice
skater for about five minutes.

00:15:41.425 --> 00:15:43.110
- With that tone. "I was
a childhood prodigy."

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:44.550
- I was not.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.331
I got a ice call from Amy
during like our break.

00:15:47.331 --> 00:15:49.260
(audience laughing)

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:50.430
- It's the pose for me.

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:53.100
That's how you know I was
serious and professional.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.440
- [Kelly] It's the visor for me. (laughs)

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:56.880
- Oh my God, that's amazing.

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:58.110
- It's like riding a bike, kinda.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:00.330
You've done it a couple
times, it starts to come back.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:03.780
I didn't break my face and
so I count that as success.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.010
- That's a win.

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:06.120
- But Amy's famous for this.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.190
She sends these texts to us once a year.

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:09.817
She'll go, I think Marin got one saying,

00:16:09.817 --> 00:16:10.800
"Do you speak French?"

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:13.140
And Marin was like, "Mhmm."
(audience laughing)

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.760
I got one saying, "Can
you play ping pong?"

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.110
I was like, "Like a pro."
(audience laughing)

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.450
- I will in a month.
- What did you get?

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:20.318
- [Kelly] Just so it
turns out I'm a champion.

00:16:20.318 --> 00:16:21.847
- "Can you bowl?"
- Mine was bowling.

00:16:21.847 --> 00:16:24.238
- "Can you ride a bike?"
I got at some point.

00:16:24.238 --> 00:16:26.370
- It was great.
- Did you have to learn how?

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:28.530
- I mean, I had always bowled, you know,

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:30.531
at birthday parties and whatnot.

00:16:30.531 --> 00:16:32.632
(Rachel laughing)

00:16:32.632 --> 00:16:33.660
Not a professional, but I did,

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:34.890
a friend of mine is
really good at bowling,

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:37.590
so he gave me a lesson and it worked.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:39.300
I mean, you know, proper form.

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.033
And it was in those Catskill scenes.

00:16:43.214 --> 00:16:46.920
In the script it says Joel bowls a strike.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:50.531
- No, you didn't do it. Oh, come on.

00:16:50.531 --> 00:16:51.891
(Michael laughing)

00:16:51.891 --> 00:16:52.894
God.
- I didn't do it.

00:16:52.894 --> 00:16:54.570
Because the lane was
warped. It was an old--

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:55.710
- Obviously.
(audience laughing)

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:58.372
- It was the lane. (laughs)

00:16:58.372 --> 00:16:59.760
- So later on when I
watched the scene back

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.080
I was like, "Oh, did I do that?"

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:03.333
And you know, they told me
no, it was special effects.

00:17:03.333 --> 00:17:05.312
(Kelly laughing)

00:17:05.312 --> 00:17:09.210
- "Am I that amazing?" "No,
we're amazing editors." (laughs)

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:12.540
So overall, what are y'all
most proud of with this show?

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:13.650
I'll start with you, Rachel.

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:15.210
- Oh man. Most proud of?

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:17.700
That we made it. (laughs)

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:18.570
- You more than made it.

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:20.070
Y'all connected with so many people.

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:21.090
Everybody loves the show.

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:23.760
- I think it's that, I've said
it a lot, but we've all been

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.510
a part of shows that we're
really proud to be a part of

00:17:28.057 --> 00:17:29.490
that just never find the audience.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:32.010
It's a lot of hard work and
a little bit of stardust

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:34.740
and we're so proud to
have been a part of it

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:37.350
and that it found its
people and that it reached

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:39.120
so many different people
for different reasons.

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:43.830
It's been so amazing over
the last five, six years

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:45.810
to meet, especially
young women who have said

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:49.530
that they felt inspired by
this character to take a risk

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.623
in their own lives, to try
stand up for the first time.

00:17:54.028 --> 00:17:58.080
To be braver and more
confident. It's just beautiful.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:17:58.913
- You're giving hope to people's dreams,

00:17:58.913 --> 00:18:01.341
like, that maybe they
put on the back burner.

00:18:01.341 --> 00:18:03.624
- And to make them laugh.
I'm proud of so many things.

00:18:03.624 --> 00:18:05.400
- Yeah. What are y'all proud
of being part of this show?

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:06.660
- I think I'm most proud
of the fact that, you know,

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:11.660
this cast, this ensemble, you
know, over the course of five,

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:16.500
six years, really,
really just stayed tight,

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:21.333
stayed respectful, checked
their egos at the door.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.740
It was literally virtually conflict free

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:27.890
which just doesn't
always happen, you know?

00:18:28.740 --> 00:18:29.940
Something's going on

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:31.950
or something's going on in someone's life

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:34.770
and then that enters into the workspace.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:39.183
But with this group, it was just a joy.

00:18:42.246 --> 00:18:45.000
I think we all should be, you
know, really proud of that.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:46.560
- Yeah. And not take it for granted.

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:48.101
I know y'all have been on other jobs,

00:18:48.101 --> 00:18:49.170
so you know that isn't always true.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.457
So it's nice when you work,
even, like, when I do, like,

00:18:51.457 --> 00:18:52.950
"The Voice" and the, the coaches, like,

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:54.540
you have like such camaraderie with them

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:57.457
and so you gotta, like, appreciate
it in the moment and go,

00:18:57.457 --> 00:18:58.680
"Ooh, it's not always like this.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.293
Like, have such a good
time and be present, yeah.

